        "problem": "$\\int (4{sin}^{2}\u2061x+6x)\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x=1-2{sin}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x=2{sin}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{sin}^{2}\u2061x+6x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\cdot 2{sin}^{2}\u2061x+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x)+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-2\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x-2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x)+6(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x+3{x}^{2}+C \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+2x-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
